<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of 1812</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1812">1812</a>–<a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of 1812 was fought from 1812 to 1815 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the United Kingdom, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s Canadian colonies and Native American allies. Provoked by British trade restrictions and the impressment of American sailors, it saw failed American invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, the burning of Washington (1814) and an American victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> (1815); the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ghent">Treaty of Ghent</a> restored the pre-war borders.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-1812.jpg" alt="War of 1812" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clockwise, from top: George Munger, John David Kelly, Anton Otto Fischer, William Emmons, Edward Percy Moran · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:War_of_1812_Montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The causes of the war have long been debated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> objected to the British orders in council of 1807, which restricted trade with Napoleonic France, and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>'s impressment of seamen from American vessels, men whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> claimed as British subjects. Britain also supported Indigenous confederacies resisting American expansion in the Northwest Territory, and some Americans may have wished to annex part or all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><p>On 1 June 1812 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> sent Congress a message setting out American grievances. The House voted 79 to 49 and the Senate 19 to 13 for war, divided on party lines, with no Federalist in favour. Madison signed the declaration on 18 June 1812, the first formal declaration of war by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, passed by the smallest margin of any in American history.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Neither side was ready. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was engaged against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, most of its army was in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a>, and it had officially 6,034 regular troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in July 1812. Its aims were defensive. American forces under William Hull crossed into Upper Canada on 12 July 1812 and reached Sandwich, but all the invasions of Canada failed. At sea the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> blockaded the American coast and raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chesapeake-bay">Chesapeake Bay</a>, while American privateers inflicted limited damage on British commerce. About 4,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> escaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> aboard British ships.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s exile in April 1814 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> reinforced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, and in August 1814 it captured and burned Washington. American victories at Baltimore and Plattsburgh in September helped end major fighting in the north. In the south, American forces and their Indigenous allies defeated the Red Stick faction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a>'s army repulsed a British attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> in January 1815, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ghent">Treaty of Ghent</a> had been signed. The treaty was agreed in December 1814 and ratified by the Senate on 17 February 1815.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty restored the pre-war territorial status quo and did not settle the maritime issues, though impressment largely ceased once the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> ended. American losses were 2,260 killed in action and about 15,000 dead from all causes related to the war, while British losses were about 1,160 killed in action. At least 10,000 Indigenous people are estimated to have died.</p><p>The Indigenous allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> lost their cause, since the proposed barrier state was rejected at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> went on to negotiate over two hundred treaties for Indigenous land. In the United States the war was called a second war of independence and the Federalist Party collapsed. Britain remembers it little. Canadians later used its memory to help build a national identity after 1867.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of 1812” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The young republic kept only a small regular army and relied on state militias, but it fought Britain again in the <b>War of 1812</b> and waged a long series of wars against Native American nations that drove them west and onto reservations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On Christmas Eve 1814 British and American negotiators at Ghent signed the Treaty of Ghent, which ended the <b>War of 1812</b> and restored the pre-war borders of June 1812.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>The Star-Spangled Banner began as a poem written on 14 September 1814, in the <b>War of 1812</b>, fitted to the tune of a London club song.</blockquote><a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>War of 1812</b>, after the burning of Washington, British forces arrested William Beanes, a physician of Upper Marlboro, Maryland, and a friend of the lawyer Francis Scott Key.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-star-spangled-banner">The Star-Spangled Banner</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>War of 1812</b> the sloop of war USS Hornet fought the brig-sloop HMS Peacock off the mouth of the Demerara River in Guyana.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-24">24 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Congress declared war on 18 June, beginning the <b>War of 1812</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-1">1 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
